 36MG    1H(36MG,36MG'G):BETA2:XUNDL-3 2014MI09                           201405
 36MG c  Compiled (unevaluated) dataset from 2014Mi09:                          
 36MG2c  Phys Rev C 89, 054307 (2014)                                           
 36MG c  Compiled by B. Singh (McMaster), May 7, 2014                           
 36MG c  Beam={+36}Mg at 44.5 MeV/nucleon, target=liquid hydrogen. Proton-proton
 36MG2c  inelastic scattering in inverse kinematics used to determine           
 36MG3c  deformation length and |b{-2} for first 2+ state                       
 36MG c  {+36}Mg produced by fragmentation of 63 MeV/nucleon {+48}Ca beam       
 36MG2c  with {+181}Ta and enriched {+64}Ni target foils of 150 |mm and         
 36MG3c  200 |mm thicknesses, respectively at RFQ-RILAC-CSM-RRC accelerator     
 36MG4c  at RIKEN facility. The fragments were separated and identified by RIPS 
 36MG5c  fragment separator optimized for A/Z=3 reaction products. Particle     
 36MG6c  identification was made from measurements of magnetic rigidity (B|r),  
 36MG7c  time-of-flight (tof), and energy loss (|DE-E). The secondary beam      
 36MG8c  of {+36}Mg at 44.5 MeV/nucleon hit a liquid hydrogen target (CRYPTA).  
 36MG9c  The scattered particles were analyzed by the TOMBEE spectrometer       
 36MGAc  consisting of a superconducting triplet quadrupole, plastic            
 36MGBc  scintillator, and a |DE-E telescope of a silicon detector and a NaI(Tl)
 36MGCc  detector. Measured E|g, I|g, |g|g-coin using DALI2 array of 160 NaI(Tl)
 36MGDc  detectors surrounding the hydrogen target. In order to apply Doppler   
 36MGEc  correction to the measured |g-ray energies, angles of the |g rays were 
 36MGFc  measured relative to the trajectories of the scattered particles. The  
 36MGGc  scattering angles were measured using two parallel-plate avalanche     
 36MGHc  counters (PPAC) upstream and one PPAC detector downstream from the     
 36MGIc  {+1}H target. Deduced deformation lengths and |b{-2} deformation       
 36MGJc  parameter from the analysis of measured angle-integrated cross section 
 36MGKc  by coupled-channel calculations with the ECIS97 computer code, and WP09
 36MGLc  global potential. Comparison with shell-model (SDPF-M) calculations,   
 36MGMc  and with previous experimental results                                 
 36MG cG $|g rays from {+35}Mg formed in neutron-removal channel were seen      
 36MG2cG (Figure 8c in 2014Mi09) at 446, 621 and 670 keV.                       
